From #51: ids of 129–256 bytes pass the dialect but get an invalid result from set_view. Pick one limit in llm_limits.h and test both sides.

Root cause: three numbers guarded the tool-call id — the dialect buffers (CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX 256, src/llm/openai_chat.c:394, src/llm/anthropic_messages.c:588), the response gate (src/assistant/response.c:106, already CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX) and the GUI tool schema (CANLIN_AV_CALL_ID_MAX 128, src/assistant/view.c:1516). An id of 129..256 bytes passed dialect + gate and was then refused by set_view with "invalid" — the gap reported in #51.

Decision: one number, 128, owned by include/canlin/llm_limits.h (CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX 256u -> 128u); include/canlin/assistant_limits.h derives CANLIN_AV_CALL_ID_MAX from it, so the GUI tool schema and the provider dialects share one bound. 128 is the GUI schema's own value (UI spec §7 states no byte bound; it was chosen for canlin#20) and it leaves src/assistant/view.c untouched — the error text there, "tool-call id must be 1-128 bytes", stays true. No spec change: the bound is "not in the spec" in both headers, and the card names llm_limits.h as its home.

Tests, red first:
- t/llm/test_llm.c test_tool_id_bound (openai-chat) and test_am_tool_id_bound (anthropic-messages): an id of exactly CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX bytes is delivered as a tool, one byte more fails the response with "full" ("tool call id exceeds CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX") and no tool is delivered.
- t/assistant/test_view.c test_call_id_bound: set_view admits an id of CANLIN_LLM_TOOL_ID_MAX bytes and refuses one byte more with EINVALID, revision unchanged. This half was red before the fix (3 checks failed: set_view refused the 256-byte id the dialect admits).

